<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Keyong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/856">856</a>–<a href="/years/908">908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Keyong was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> Turkic military leader in the last decades of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. His cavalry drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a>'s rebels from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 883, and the Tang made him Prince of Jin, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>. He was the great rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> destroyed the Liang and founded the Later Tang in 923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-keyong.jpg" alt="Li Keyong" width="320" height="670" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Keyong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Keyong, who first bore the surname Zhuye, was born on 24 October 856, the third son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> chieftain Zhuye Chixin and Lady <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, among a people then living by the Shenwu River in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>. In 869 his father led Shatuo soldiers for the Tang general Kang Chengxun against the rebellion of Pang Xun, and the fourteen-year-old fought so fiercely that he was called the Son of the Flying Tiger. Emperor Yizong gave his father the imperial surname Li and made him military governor of Zhenwu Circuit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 878, as deputy commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a>, Li Keyong joined a mutiny, executed the defender Duan Wenchu and claimed his post, which Emperor Xizong refused to grant. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, the court pardoned him in 881, and he returned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dada">Dada</a> with 10,000 of their soldiers. In 884 he relieved Chen Prefecture, which Huang had besieged for some 300 days, and crushed Huang's army at the Wangman Crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. That campaign began his enmity with Zhu Quanzhong, the military governor of Xuanwu.</p><p>Under Emperor Zhaozong, who made him an honorary chancellor, he took the Zhaoyi prefectures of Meng Fangli in 889–890 through his adoptive son Li Cunxiao, and in 891 starved Helian Duo out of Yun Prefecture and took Datong Circuit. In 895 he backed Wang Ke's claim to Hezhong against Wang Gong and his allies Wang Xingyu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-maozhen">Li Maozhen</a>. From January 896 he was Prince of Jin, but that year Luo Hongxin of Weibo, angered by the pillaging of Li Keyong's troops, turned to Zhu Quanzhong and closed his territory to them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Zhu Quanzhong ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> in 907 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, Li Keyong kept using the Tang era names. His state of Jin was in practice independent, though he refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jian-former-shu">Wang Jian</a>'s suggestion that he take an imperial title, claiming continued loyalty to the Tang. He met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Yelü Abaoji</a>, ruler of the Khitan, at Yun Prefecture to seek an alliance against Later Liang, but Yelü later dealt with the Liang instead.</p><p>He died on 24 February 908 and was succeeded as Prince of Jin by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, his son by his concubine Lady Cao. Among his adoptive sons was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Li Siyuan</a>, later Emperor Mingzong of Later Tang. A Ming novel attributed to Luo Guanzhong centres on his thirteen grand-guardians, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> films and television have retold it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Keyong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Li Keyong</b>, chief of the Shatuo Turks, helped the Tang drive Huang Chao out of Chang'an in 883 and was rewarded with the governorship of Hedong, centred on Taiyuan.</blockquote><a href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a></li><li><blockquote>His general Zhu Wen defected to the Tang in 882, and in 883 the Shatuo cavalry of <b>Li Keyong</b> drove Huang Chao from Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/state/qi-huang-chao">Qi (Huang Chao)</a></li></ul></section>
